USER # Evaluator Driven — the custody record
USER 
USER **Version 0.9.8 · candidate · 2026-11-04** · Predicate type `https://evaluatordriven.com/custody/v1`
USER Author: Steve Moitozo II · Text: CC BY 4.0 · Code: Apache-2.0 · Patents: see `PATENTS.md`
USER 
USER Sections marked *(normative)* define conformance. Everything else explains it.
USER 
USER ## Why this name *(non-normative)*
USER 
USER If you want acceptable output from work with AI, you have to be able to show that the output meets a
USER standard an independent evaluator can check, and that standard has to be informed by two people: the
USER person who wants the output, and a specialist who knows the craft.
USER 
USER Evaluator-driven because the evaluator, and the standard it applies, come first. Build them first and the
USER work can be aimed at the standard. Build them afterward and you have a description, not a standard.
USER 
USER ---
USER 
USER ## 0. Status
USER 
USER Early. One reference checker exists, in this repository. The evidence base is narrow and its limits are
USER listed in `README.md`. Comments and failing records are welcome under `CONTRIBUTING.md`.
USER 
USER ---
USER 
USER ## 1. Scope *(normative)*
USER 
USER This specification defines the **custody record**: a record kept with a piece of work that shows what the
USER work was measured against, who measured it, what they found, and who decided.
USER 
USER It standardizes the record, not the process that produced the work. How work is made, revised or
USER iterated is out of scope.
USER 
USER A conforming record makes work **answerable, not correct**. No requirement here claims, or may be read
USER as claiming, that conforming work is good.
USER 
USER A signature or content credential on the artifact describes where the artifact came from. It is never an
USER outcome for a dimension.
USER 
USER ---
USER 
USER ## 2. Terms *(normative)*
USER 
USER The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT",
USER "RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this document are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14
USER (RFC 2119, RFC 8174) when, and only when, they appear in all capitals.
USER 
USER | Term | Meaning |
USER |---|---|
USER | **artifact** | The work being evaluated, identified by the digests in the record's `subject`. |
USER | **bar** | The written statement of what good means for the artifact: its dimensions and, for each, what conforming looks like. Frozen before the work starts. |
USER | **dimension** | One part of the bar, judged on its own. |
USER | **maker** | The party that produced the artifact. |
USER | **evaluator** | A person, a model session, or a script that reads the artifact against the bar. An evaluator did not make the artifact and did not author the bar. Each session of a model is a separate party, so a session of the maker's own model can evaluate at CL1 if the record declares it (CL1-6); at CL2 it may sit alongside other evaluators, provided at least one model evaluator differs from the maker in both family and endpoint (CL2-3). |
USER | **buyer** | The party the work is for. The buyer's test is whether the work does the job it was meant to do. |
USER | **specialist** | The party that knows the field well enough to say what good means. |
USER | **check** | One evaluator's reading of one dimension in one run. Its outcome is `conforms`, `does_not_conform`, or `unobserved`. |
USER | **instrument** | The written rubric an evaluator reads by, and the runner that applies it. |
USER | **record** | The custody record defined here: an in-toto Statement whose predicate is `custody/v1`. |
USER | **published** | Made available at a stable address to the people who rely on the record. Published need not mean public. |
USER | **disposition** | The decision about the artifact — `given`, `not_yet_given`, or `declined` — made by a named person, the approver. |
USER 
USER **Plain-language equivalents** used on evaluatordriven.com:
USER 
USER | This specification | evaluatordriven.com |
USER |---|---|
USER | specialist | subject matter expert |
USER | `unobserved` | not observed |
USER | `given` / `not_yet_given` / `declined` | accepted / not yet accepted / declined |
USER 
USER ---
USER 
USER ## 3. The four moves
USER 
USER The record carries evidence of four moves. Section 5 states what each requires.
USER 
USER | Move | What the record shows | Requirements |
USER |---|---|---|
USER | Set the bar | The bar existed, unchanged, before the work started; its buyer and specialist approved that exact bar | CL1-1 to CL1-3 |
USER | Produce | Who made the work, by what method, against which bar, with which instrument | CL1-4, CL1-5 |
USER | Evaluate independently | Who read it, against what, with what outcome, beside a control | CL1-6 to CL1-10, CL1-15 to CL1-17 |
USER | Keep the record | What failed, what separation was missing, where the record lives, who decided | CL1-11 to CL1-14 |
USER 
USER ---
USER 
USER ## 4. The record *(normative)*
USER 
USER A record MUST be an in-toto Statement v1 whose `predicateType` is
USER `https://evaluatordriven.com/custody/v1` and whose `predicate` has the fields below. A field listed as
USER required MUST be present; a required list MAY be empty only where this table says so. Absence never
USER means a default.
USER 
USER | Field | Type | Required | Notes |
USER |---|---|---|---|
USER | `specVersion` | string | yes | This document's version, e.g. `0.9.8` |
USER | `levelClaimed` | `CL1` · `CL2` · `CL3` · `baseline` | yes | |
USER | `bar.digest` | digest | yes | sha256 of the frozen bar document |
USER | `bar.frozenAt` | timestamp | yes | RFC 3339, UTC |
USER | `bar.author` | string | yes | |
USER | `bar.dimensions[]` | list, non-empty | yes | `id`, `criterion`; `scale` when scored |
USER | `bar.dimensions[].scale` | object or `null` | yes | `anchors`, `threshold`, `floor` |
USER | `bar.approvals[]` | list | yes | `role` (`buyer`·`specialist`), `by`, `barDigest`, `at`, `adopted` |
USER | `bar.publishedAt` | URI or `null` | yes | |
USER | `bar.groundTruth[]` | list, may be empty | yes | `name`, `version`, `digest` — each reference the bar judges against |
USER | `bar.dissent[]` | list, may be empty | yes | `by`, `note` — where the buyer and specialist disagree about the bar, recorded, never averaged |
USER | `maker` | object | yes | `name`, `kind` (`person`·`model`·`script`), `version`, `method` (digest), `model` (or `null`) |
USER | `startedAt` | timestamp | yes | When work on the artifact began |
USER | `producedAt` | timestamp | yes | When the artifact was finished |
USER | `readsBeforeFreeze[]` | list, may be empty | only in a `baseline` claim; BL-1 checks it | Not required, and not read, in any other record |
USER | `instrument` | object | yes | `rubric` (digest), `runner` (digest) |
USER | `evaluators[]` | list, non-empty | yes | `id`, `name`, `kind` (`person`·`model`·`script`), `model` (or `null`); `script` (`name`, `author`, `digest`) when `kind` is `script`; optional `sameModelAsMaker` (boolean) |
USER | `checks[]` | list, non-empty | yes | `dimension`, `evaluator`, `run` (1 or more), `batch`, `at`, `outcome`, `score`, `failureCondition`, `evidence` (an evidence file, or `null`), `unobserved`; optional `endpoint` |
USER | `controls[]` | list, non-empty | yes | `id`, `batch`, `evaluator`, `case` (digest), `expected`, `outcome` |
USER | `spread[]` | list | in a CL2 or CL3 claim | `dimension`, `evaluator`, `runs`, `agreement` |
USER | `disagreements[]` | list, may be empty | yes | `dimension`, `checks` (indexes into `checks[]`), `note` |
USER | `violations[]` | list, may be empty | yes | `dimension`, `description` |
USER | `separationGaps[]` | list, may be empty | yes | strings |
USER | `retention` | object | yes | `location`, `public` (boolean) |
USER | `demonstrations[]` | list | in a CL3 claim | `dimension`, `evaluator`, `planted` (digest), `outcome`, `at` |
USER | `external[]` | list, may be empty | yes | `requirement` (`CL1-15`, or its 0.x name `CL2-4`; `CL3-3`), `subject` (the evaluator's `id` for CL1-15; `record` for CL3-3), `evidence` (an evidence file), `readBy` |
USER | `disposition` | object | yes | `state`, `by`, `at`, `acknowledgesUnobserved[]` |
USER 
USER Every check in a record is read against `bar.digest`; a reading against any other bar belongs in another
USER record. A filed check is not edited: a correction is a new check with a later `at`, and both stay in
USER `checks`. A record is plain JSON, readable without any tool.
USER 
USER A **digest** is an object `{"sha256": "<64 lowercase hex>"}`. An **evidence file** is an object
USER `{"name": "<relative path>", "sha256": "<64 lowercase hex>"}`, naming a file in the evidence a verifier is
USER given. `model`, wherever it appears, is `null` or an object with `family`, `identifier`, `endpoint`, and
USER `readOnly` (boolean). `family` is the model line as its builder names it, in lowercase letters and
USER hyphens only, with no version (for example `gpt` or `qwen`), or `undisclosed` when the builder does not
USER say. `identifier` is the exact model version. `endpoint` is the host name of the service that answered,
USER in lowercase with at least one dot (for example `api.example.com`); a router that passed the request on
USER is not the service that answered. A check MAY carry its own `endpoint` when the host that answered it
USER differs from its evaluator's. A `control` may also carry a `dimension`, naming the one dimension it tests.
USER 
USER A **scale** (`bar.dimensions[].scale`) has `anchors`, an object that keys a description by score (for
USER example `{"1": "no figure cites a source", "5": "every figure cites its source"}`); a `floor`, the lowest
USER score on the scale; and a `threshold`, the score a check needs to conform.
USER 
USER **Extensions.** A producer MAY add its own fields anywhere in the predicate under keys that begin with
USER `x-`. No requirement reads them, and a verifier ignores them. *(Non-normative: a record re-filed from an
USER earlier instrument keeps its original times and can carry the earlier verdict under an `x-` key; it
USER conforms only on its own fields.)*
USER 
USER `schema/custody-v1.schema.json` encodes this table for JSON Schema 2020-12 validators. Where the schema
USER and this section differ, this section governs.
USER 
USER ---
USER 
USER ## 5. Requirements *(normative)*
USER 
USER A record MUST meet CR-1, CR-2, and every requirement of the level it claims and of every level below it.
USER A record that claims `baseline` MUST meet CR-1, CR-2, BL-1, CL1-2 to CL1-14, CL1-16 and CL1-17. BL-1
USER applies only to a record that claims `baseline`: a record that claims CL1, CL2 or CL3 is not held to it,
USER and a verifier does not report it for that record.
USER 
USER Each requirement has a stable ID and a **check class**:
USER 
USER - **structural** — decidable from the record's shape alone;
USER - **semantic** — decidable from the record's content, and from supplied files where noted;
USER - **external** — needs evidence outside the record, read by a named party.
USER 
USER **When evidence is missing:** a structural or semantic requirement that the record breaks reads
USER `FAILED`. A semantic requirement that needs files no one supplied reads `NOT-CHECKABLE`. An external
USER requirement reads `OK` only when its evidence file is supplied, its digest matches, and `readBy` names a
USER party who is not the maker (and, for CL3-3, the verifier has verified the bundle itself); a mismatched digest reads `FAILED`; evidence read only by the maker is
USER self-report and reads `NOT-CHECKABLE`; a named file that was not supplied reads `NOT-CHECKABLE`. When the record names **no** evidence at all, the two
USER external requirements differ: independence (CL1-15) is a claim about the world, so an unevidenced claim
USER reads `NOT-CHECKABLE`; a signature (CL3-3) is a property of the record, so its absence reads `FAILED`. A
USER field that only asserts independence never satisfies a requirement on its own.
USER 
USER **One defect, one failure.** When a field is missing or malformed, the requirement that checks that
USER field reads `FAILED`: CR-2, or the requirement this section names as checking the field itself. Any
USER other requirement that cannot be decided without that field reads `NOT-CHECKABLE`. *(Non-normative
USER example: a check whose `evidence` is `{"sha256": "…"}`, with no `name`, does not cite an evidence file.
USER CR-2 reads `FAILED`; CL1-8, which cannot look for a file that is not named, reads `NOT-CHECKABLE`.)*
USER 
USER ### 5.1 Every record
USER 
USER | ID | Requirement | Class |
USER |---|---|---|
USER | CR-1 | The record is an in-toto Statement v1: `_type` is `https://in-toto.io/Statement/v1`; `subject` is non-empty and every entry has a `name` and a sha256 digest; `predicateType` is exactly `https://evaluatordriven.com/custody/v1`. | structural |
USER | CR-2 | Every field section 4 marks `yes` is present with its stated type and form (a `family`, an `endpoint`, a `run`), and so are `spread` in a CL2 or CL3 claim and `demonstrations` in a CL3 claim. CR-2 does not check the following fields, because the requirement named beside each checks it itself: each dimension's `id`, `criterion` and `scale` (CL1-2); `maker` (CL1-4); `instrument`, and each script evaluator's `script` (CL1-5); `violations` and `separationGaps` (CL1-11); `retention` (CL1-12); `readsBeforeFreeze` (BL-1, and only in a `baseline` claim). | structural |
USER 
USER ### 5.2 CL1 — the four moves
USER 
USER | ID | Requirement | Class |
USER |---|---|---|
USER | CL1-1 | `bar.frozenAt` is earlier than `startedAt`, and `startedAt` is no later than `producedAt`. A record whose bar was frozen after the work started can claim only `baseline` (BL-1). A bar frozen at the same instant the work started fits neither: it is not earlier, and it is not after. | semantic |
USER | CL1-2 | The bar names its author and at least one dimension. Every dimension has a unique `id` and a `criterion`. A dimension whose checks carry scores declares a `scale` with `anchors`, a `threshold`, and a `floor`. A scale's anchors are keyed by score, there is at least one, none is below the `floor`, and the `threshold` lies between the `floor` and the highest anchor. | structural |
USER | CL1-3 | `bar.approvals` holds one `buyer` and one `specialist` approval, each naming who approved, when, and a `barDigest` equal to `bar.digest`. A specialist that is a published document is recorded with `adopted` naming the document and its version (a shape CR-2 checks), and `by` naming the person who adopted it. | semantic |
USER | CL1-4 | The maker is named with its `kind` and a version, and `maker.method` is the digest of the written rules used to make the artifact. | structural |
USER | CL1-5 | The instrument is pinned: `instrument.rubric` and `instrument.runner` are digests, and every evaluator of kind `script` names its script, its script's author, and its digest. | structural |
USER | CL1-6 | No evaluator is the maker or the bar's author, and no script evaluator's script was written by either. The approver is not an evaluator. A model evaluator carries a `model` block. A model evaluator whose `identifier` equals the maker's declares `sameModelAsMaker: true`, and one whose `identifier` differs does not. For a person, this shows separately named parties, not proven independence; a record holder who needs more attaches evidence. | semantic |
USER | CL1-7 | Every dimension on the bar has at least one check. A dimension with no check fails the record: its evidence is not attributable. | semantic |
USER | CL1-8 | Every check names a dimension on the bar and a listed evaluator, gives a run, batch, and time, and declares its `failureCondition` — what would have made it return `does_not_conform`. A `conforms` or `does_not_conform` outcome cites an evidence file, and the file supplied to the verifier matches its digest: a mismatch reads `FAILED`, and a file not supplied reads `NOT-CHECKABLE`. An `unobserved` outcome names the channel tried and why it could not observe, and carries no score. | semantic |
USER | CL1-9 | In every batch, each evaluator with a check that is not `unobserved` reads at least two controls there: one whose expected outcome is `conforms`, and one whose expected outcome is `does_not_conform`. Every control records its expected and actual outcome, and its `case` is the digest of the case it ran on, never all zeros. A control is a case whose correct outcome is known in advance. A control whose actual outcome differs from its expected outcome shows its reader could not be relied on: every check by that evaluator in that batch reads `unobserved`, or, if the control names a `dimension`, every such check of that dimension. | semantic |
USER | CL1-10 | Where checks of one dimension return different outcomes, `disagreements` names that dimension and the checks. Outcomes are never averaged into one. | semantic |
USER | CL1-11 | Every dimension with a `does_not_conform` check appears in `violations`. `violations` and `separationGaps` are present even when empty. If the maker wrote the bar (`bar.author`), or approved it as buyer or specialist, an entry in `separationGaps` names the maker. | semantic |
USER | CL1-12 | `retention` states where the full record is kept and whether it is public. A record need not be public; it is kept, and it can be produced on request. | structural |
USER | CL1-13 | The disposition is `given`, `not_yet_given`, or `declined`, with the approver's name and time. If any check is `unobserved`, the record's overall result is not "all conform", and a `given` disposition lists each unobserved dimension in `acknowledgesUnobserved`. | semantic |
USER | CL1-14 | Each `subject` digest matches the supplied artifact file of that name, and `bar.digest` matches the supplied bar file. Without those files this reads `NOT-CHECKABLE`. | semantic |
