USER ## 6. Verifying a record *(normative)*
USER 
USER A verifier is given the record, the artifact's files, the bar file, and the evidence files the record names. It reports,
USER for each requirement in scope, `OK`, `FAILED`, or `NOT-CHECKABLE`, and then (the requirements in scope are
USER those section 5 says the claimed level must meet: a `baseline` claim is not held to CL1-1 or CL1-15, nor
USER to any CL2 or CL3 requirement; a CL1, CL2 or CL3 claim is not held to BL-1):
USER 
USER - **verdict** — `FAILED` if any requirement failed; otherwise `NOT-CHECKABLE` if any could not be checked;
USER   otherwise `OK`;
USER - **level achieved** — the highest claimed-or-lower level whose requirements, and those of every level
USER   below it, are all `OK`; `baseline` when a baseline claim passes; `none` otherwise;
USER - **routing** — `to approver` when the disposition is `not_yet_given`, or when a check is `unobserved` and
USER   no `given` disposition acknowledges every unobserved dimension; `acknowledged` when a check is
USER   `unobserved` and a `given` disposition acknowledges every unobserved dimension; otherwise `none`.
USER 
USER A verifier MUST NOT report a level the record does not claim, and MUST NOT turn an `unobserved` outcome
USER into any other outcome.
USER 
USER `checker/check.py` is a reference verifier. It is not normative: where it and this document differ, this
USER document governs, and the difference is a bug in the checker.
USER 
