USER ## 7. Security and privacy considerations *(normative)*
USER 
USER **The record can be separated from the artifact.** Kept beside the work, a record can be dropped by
USER anyone who copies the work. CL1-14 binds the record to the artifact by digest; CL3-3 adds a signature
USER and a transparency-log entry so a stripped record can be found again.
USER 
USER **Held-out cases stay held out.** At CL3 the record carries only the digest of each planted case. A
USER planted case published with the record teaches the next maker to write to it. "Held out" means held out
USER from the maker of this record: a control or planted case that this record's maker never saw is held out
USER for this record, whoever else has seen it. The same holds for CL1 controls.
USER 
USER **A control shows what a reader caught, not how hard the case was.** A verifier confirms that each control
USER was read and that its outcome matched what was expected. Whether a control could have been missed is a
USER judgment no verifier can make from the record.
USER 
USER **The custody log has no outside anchor.** `tools/record.py` chains each event to the one before it, so an
USER edited, removed or reordered event inside a log is caught. A log rewritten from its first event, or cut
USER short at its end, still verifies. Signing the record (CL3-3) is what fixes it in place.
USER 
USER **Signing publishes the signer.** A transparency log is public and permanent: a record signed for CL3-3
USER puts the signer's identity in that log for good.
USER 
USER **Publication is optional.** CL1-12 requires the record be kept and producible, not public. Evidence can
USER name people and contain confidential work; a record holder MAY withhold evidence files and publish only
USER digests. A verifier without them reads CL1-8 and CL1-15 as `NOT-CHECKABLE`.
USER 
USER **Names are personal data.** `by`, `author`, and `name` fields identify people. Record holders are
USER responsible for the law that applies to them.
USER 
